        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Natural stone is porous, and porosity is the whole problem. Oil, wine, leaf tannins and mud do not sit on stone the way they sit on a tile — they soak in, and once they are in, they are a restoration job rather than a cleaning one. A sealer does not make stone bulletproof. It buys you the time to wipe the spill up.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="do-you-need-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Does Your Stone Actually Need Sealing?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Not all stone does, and sealing something that does not need it wastes money and can leave residue on the surface. The test takes five minutes and settles the question completely.
        </p>

        <p>
          Put a few drops of water on the stone in several places — a traffic path, an edge, under a mat if there is one. Wait five minutes and look. If the stone has darkened where the water sat, it is absorbing, and it will absorb oil and wine just as readily. If the drops are still sitting proud and the stone is unchanged underneath, whatever is on there is still working and adding more sealer achieves nothing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Porosity varies enormously across the stones people call natural stone. Dense granite absorbs very little and may need sealing rarely or never. Limestone, travertine, sandstone and unfilled flagstone are thirsty and benefit substantially. Slate sits in the middle, and honed marble absorbs far more readily than polished marble because the surface has been opened up by the finish.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Sealing Buys You</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5 min</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water test to know if you need it</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-5 yr</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical interval, by porosity</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protection against etching</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">15 min</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wipe back excess within this</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="penetrating-vs-topical" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Penetrating vs Topical: Choose the Category First
        </h2>

        <p>
          Almost everyone should be buying a penetrating sealer, and the shelf does not make that obvious.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Penetrating sealers</strong> — also sold as impregnators — soak into the pore structure and line it with a repellent layer. Nothing remains on the surface, so there is no film to peel, no change in appearance, no loss of traction, and no stripping cycle when it eventually wears. They protect against staining while letting the stone breathe, which matters on anything with moisture behind it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Topical sealers</strong> form a film on the surface, usually acrylic. They deliver gloss and colour enhancement, which is their only real advantage, and they pay for it: films wear in traffic paths, cloud when moisture gets underneath, can become slippery, and eventually need stripping rather than simply recoating. Outdoors on natural stone they are rarely the right answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          The exception is the wet look. If the saturated, rain-soaked appearance is what you actually want, a penetrating silane-siloxane enhancer like the Ghostshield product above gets you there far more durably than an acrylic topcoat. Just be sure before you start — the change is not something you undo on a weekend.
        </p>

        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
