        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What About Enhancers and "Colour Enhancing" Sealers?</h3>
        <p>
          A third category sits between the two: enhancing penetrating sealers, which soak in like an impregnator but carry an oil or resin that deepens the stone's colour permanently. They are not the same as a topical gloss — there is no film, so traction and breathability are preserved — but they do change the look, and that change is effectively permanent until the product wears away over years.
        </p>
        <p>
          They earn their place on stone that has gone flat and grey with age, particularly slate and older flagstone, where an enhancer restores something close to the colour the stone had when it was laid. They are a poor choice on pale limestone, where the darkening reads as dirt rather than richness. As with any enhancer, test a hidden area and look at it dry, in daylight, before committing to the whole floor.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-stone" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. By Stone Type
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Limestone</h3>
        <p>
          Porous, calcium-based and the stone that benefits most from sealing. It absorbs readily, stains easily from oil and wine, and etches on contact with anything acidic. A penetrating sealer is essential on limestone worktops and floors, and worth repeating annually in a kitchen. Outdoors, limestone paving also takes up leaf tannins and algae staining, so a heavy-duty exterior sealer earns its price.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Travertine</h3>
        <p>
          Limestone's holey cousin. Filled travertine seals like limestone; unfilled travertine has open voids that trap dirt and need a sealer that can get into them without pooling. Around a pool, the extra consideration is slip resistance — stay penetrating, never topical.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slate and Flagstone</h3>
        <p>
          Variable, and worth testing rather than assuming. Some slate is nearly impervious; some flagstone drinks like a sponge. Both are usually outdoors, taking weather and traffic, which argues for a durable exterior penetrating sealer. This is also the category where the wet-look decision comes up most, because saturated slate is genuinely beautiful.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Marble and Granite</h3>
        <p>
          Both are covered in more depth in our <a href="/best-marble-sealer/">marble sealer</a> and <a href="/best-granite-sealer/">granite sealer</a> guides, because both have their own quirks — marble's vulnerability to etching, granite's density and the resin treatments applied at the factory. As a rule, honed finishes need sealing more than polished ones, and darker granites often need it far less than pale ones.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sandstone</h3>
        <p>
          Among the most porous materials people pave with, and one where sealing makes a visible difference within a season. It is also prone to spalling in freeze-thaw climates, which a good water-repellent sealer helps slow by keeping water out of the pore structure in the first place.
        </p>

