        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoors vs Outdoors</h3>
        <p>
          The same stone in two locations is effectively two different jobs. Indoors, the threats are oil, wine, coffee and citrus, the surface is dry, and appearance matters at close range — which favours a low-odour water-based product applied carefully and reapplied on a short cycle. Outdoors, the threats are freeze-thaw, leaf tannins, algae, sun and mud, the stone is regularly saturated, and nobody inspects it from twelve inches away. That favours a heavier-duty solvent-carried sealer with real weather durability, applied in a settled dry spell.
        </p>
        <p>
          One consequence catches people out: a sealer that performed beautifully on a kitchen floor can underwhelm on a patio of the same limestone, not because the product failed but because the exposure is an order of magnitude harsher. Buy for the location rather than for the stone alone.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. How to Apply a Stone Sealer
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean first, and thoroughly.</strong> A sealer locks in whatever is on the stone. Use a pH-neutral stone cleaner rather than a general household product — acidic cleaners etch calcium-based stone, and alkaline ones can leave residue that blocks absorption.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry completely.</strong> Twenty-four hours minimum after cleaning, longer for dense stone or humid conditions. Pores full of water cannot take up sealer, and the result is patchy protection you will not notice until something stains.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Test an inconspicuous area.</strong> Especially with any enhancing or wet-look product, and especially on stone you cannot replace. A day's patience beats a permanent surprise across a whole floor.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply thin and even.</strong> A microfibre applicator, a lambswool pad, or a low-pressure sprayer, depending on the area. Work in manageable sections so you can get back to wipe them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wipe back the excess.</strong> Within the window on the label, usually five to fifteen minutes, buff off everything that has not soaked in. This single step separates a professional-looking result from a hazy one, and it is the step people skip because the surface looks fine while it is still wet.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Second coat if the stone is thirsty.</strong> Porous stone often wants two. Follow the recoat window on the label rather than waiting a week, and repeat the wipe-back.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep the room ventilated and stay off it.</strong> Solvent-based sealers need airflow during application and while curing, and every product needs the surface left alone for the cure time on the label — usually a few hours before light foot traffic and considerably longer before rugs, furniture or anything that traps moisture against the stone goes back down.
        </p>

        <h2 id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Resealing and Maintenance
        </h2>

        <p>
          Forget the calendar and use the water test. Sealer wears at the rate the surface is used, so a doorway wears years before the corner of the same floor, and spot-resealing a traffic path is entirely legitimate.
        </p>

        <p>
          Between seals, the biggest factor in how stone ages is what you clean it with. pH-neutral cleaners only: vinegar, lemon-based cleaners and most bathroom products are acidic and will etch limestone, travertine and marble regardless of any sealer. Grit is the other enemy on floors — a doormat and regular sweeping do more for a stone floor's life than any product on this page.
        </p>

        <p>
          Outdoors, clear organic debris promptly. Leaves left on limestone paving through a wet autumn leave tannin stains that outlast the leaves by years, and algae takes hold in exactly the same conditions. A stiff brush and a rinse a few times a season prevents both.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dealing With an Existing Stain</h3>
        <p>
          Sealing does nothing for a stain that is already in the stone, and applying sealer over one locks it in. Oil-based stains — cooking oil, grease, some cosmetics — come out with a poultice: a paste of an absorbent powder and a solvent, spread over the mark, covered, and left to draw the stain back out over a day or two. It often takes several rounds, and it works far better than any amount of scrubbing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Organic stains from leaves, coffee or wine respond better to a hydrogen peroxide poultice, and rust marks need a dedicated rust remover formulated for stone rather than a general one, which can etch. Whatever the treatment, the sequence matters: remove the stain, let the stone dry completely, then seal. Doing it the other way around means living with the mark for as long as the sealer lasts.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Dirty Stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealer preserves whatever is on the surface when it goes on. Clean with a pH-neutral stone cleaner, rinse, and let it dry through — anything else is sealing the stains in permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Excess to Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Anything not absorbed within the label's window dries as a haze or a sticky film, and removing it is a far bigger job than wiping it back would have been. Work in sections you can return to in time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Sealer to Prevent Etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Etching is a chemical reaction between acid and calcium-based stone, not absorption. No sealer prevents it. Wipe citrus, wine and vinegar promptly, and keep acidic cleaners away from marble, limestone and travertine entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Topical Sealer Outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic films outdoors cloud when moisture gets beneath them, wear in traffic paths, and eventually need stripping. If you want a wet look outside, use a penetrating enhancer instead of a surface coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on a Schedule Rather Than a Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer applied over sealer that is still working simply sits on the surface and hazes. Five minutes with a few drops of water tells you what a calendar cannot.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best natural stone sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Miracle Sealants 511 Impregnator for most situations — it works across almost every stone indoors and out, changes nothing about the appearance, and lasts for years. Black Diamond is the low-odour water-based alternative for interior work, and StoneTech BulletProof is the stronger choice for exterior stone taking weather and traffic.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if stone needs sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Drop water on it and wait five minutes. If the stone darkens, it is absorbing and wants sealing. If the drops still bead and nothing has changed underneath, the existing protection is working. Test several areas — wear is never uniform.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should it be resealed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">By porosity and use, not the calendar. Dense granite can go five years or more; a porous limestone worktop in a busy kitchen may want it annually. Exterior stone typically falls between one and three years. The water test decides it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing stop staining completely?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. It reduces absorption, which buys time to wipe a spill before it soaks in. It does nothing about etching, which is a chemical reaction between acids and calcium-based stones like marble, limestone and travertine.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Penetrating or topical?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Penetrating for nearly everything: it soaks in, leaves no film, cannot peel, and does not change appearance or traction. Topical is only worth it when gloss or a wet look is the specific goal — and outdoors those films wear, cloud and eventually need stripping.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal limestone and travertine?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, and both benefit more than most stones because they are porous and calcium-based. A penetrating sealer meaningfully slows staining on each. Neither can be protected from etching, so acidic spills still need wiping promptly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What if you over-apply?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Excess that cannot absorb dries as a hazy or sticky residue that is much harder to remove than to prevent. Apply thinly, allow the absorption time on the label, and buff back everything still on the surface before it dries.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is stone sealer safe on worktops?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Water-based sealers sold for countertops are formulated to be food-safe once fully cured, which is why they suit worktops. Observe the cure time on the label before preparing food on the surface, and ventilate while applying regardless of the low odour.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

